<DocsLayout title="Install and setup" description="Install PGSandbox, start a local Postgres sandbox, configure your coding agent’s MCP client, and verify setup with doctor and a disposable smoke test.">
  <h1>Install and setup</h1>
  <p>
    PGSandbox is a local Rust binary for MCP clients. By default it manages
    a local Postgres cluster under <code>~/.pgsandbox/</code> and chooses a high
    port such as <code>65432</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The managed local runtime requires <code>initdb</code>,
    <code>pg_ctl</code>, and <code>postgres</code> on <code>PATH</code>. The
    <code>clone_database</code> MCP tool additionally requires
    <code>pg_dump</code> and <code>pg_restore</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Agent-assisted setup</h2>
  <p>Copy this prompt into your coding agent if you want it to install and configure PGSandbox for you:</p>
  <pre class="shell"><code>{agentSetupPrompt}</code></pre>

  <h2>Homebrew</h2>
  <pre class="shell"><code>brew install LVTD-LLC/tap/pgsandbox
pgsandbox setup --client codex
pgsandbox doctor</code></pre>
  <p>
    This uses the <a href="https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/homebrew-tap">LVTD-LLC/homebrew-tap</a>
    repository, which Homebrew addresses as <code>LVTD-LLC/tap</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>From source</h2>
  <pre class="shell"><code>cargo install --path .
pgsandbox setup --client codex
pgsandbox doctor</code></pre>

  <h2>Update</h2>
  <p>
    The installed CLI binary is the MCP server process that clients launch.
    Updating the CLI and restarting the MCP client updates the server. Rerun
    <code>setup</code> when the binary path, explicit admin URL, selected client,
    or scope changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Homebrew can only upgrade after a newer GitHub release exists and the
    <code>LVTD-LLC/homebrew-tap</code> formula has been updated.
  </p>

  <h3>Homebrew</h3>
  <pre class="shell"><code>brew update
brew upgrade LVTD-LLC/tap/pgsandbox
pgsandbox --version
pgsandbox setup --client codex
pgsandbox doctor</code></pre>

  <h3>GitHub install script</h3>
  <pre class="shell"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LVTD-LLC/pgsandbox/main/scripts/install.sh | sh
pgsandbox --version
pgsandbox setup --client codex
pgsandbox doctor</code></pre>

  <h3>Custom install path</h3>
  <pre class="shell"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LVTD-LLC/pgsandbox/main/scripts/install.sh | PGSANDBOX_INSTALL_DIR=/usr/local/bin sh
pgsandbox setup --client codex --command /usr/local/bin/pgsandbox</code></pre>

  <h3>Source install</h3>
  <pre class="shell"><code>cargo install --path . --force
# or, from GitHub:
cargo install --git https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/pgsandbox --tag v&lt;VERSION&gt; --force</code></pre>
  <p>Replace <code>v&lt;VERSION&gt;</code> with the release tag you want to install.</p>

  <h2>Supported clients</h2>
  <pre class="shell"><code>pgsandbox setup --client codex
pgsandbox setup --client cursor --scope project
pgsandbox setup --client vscode --scope project
pgsandbox setup --client claude-desktop
pgsandbox setup --client all</code></pre>

  <h2 id="cursor-postgres-mcp">Set up a Postgres MCP server in Cursor</h2>
  <p>
    Use PGSandbox when Cursor needs a disposable PostgreSQL database for a
    migration, SQL check, or test—not access to your production database.
    Cursor launches the local <code>pgsandbox mcp</code> process over stdio;
    PGSandbox manages the sandbox database lifecycle.
  </p>
  <h3>1. Choose project or user configuration</h3>
  <p>
    After installing the binary above, run this from the repository you open
    in Cursor. Project scope writes <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in the current
    directory. It does not create a separate Postgres cluster for each repository.
  </p>
  <pre class="shell"><code>pgsandbox --version
pgsandbox setup --client cursor --scope project
pgsandbox doctor
pgsandbox smoke-test</code></pre>
  <p>
    For configuration shared across your projects, use
    <code>pgsandbox setup --client cursor --scope user</code> instead; it writes
    <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>. Pick the scope you intend to use rather than
    configuring both while troubleshooting. Setup may install missing PostgreSQL
    binaries when a supported package manager is available. If that fails,
    install the required server binaries and rerun setup.
  </p>
  <p>
    These commands and paths match PGSandbox v0.5.0 and the current
    <a href="https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/pgsandbox/blob/v0.5.0/rust-src/setup.rs">setup implementation</a>.
    See <a href="https://cursor.com/docs/mcp">Cursor’s MCP documentation</a>
    for its project/user configuration and tool-approval controls.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Restart Cursor and check the server</h3>
  <p>
    Restart Cursor, open the intended repository, and check that its MCP
    settings list <code>pgsandbox</code> and expose its tools. A successful
    <code>doctor</code> or <code>smoke-test</code> checks the local runtime;
    it does not prove that Cursor has loaded the configuration. Keep tool
    approvals enabled while checking the first workflow.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Ask for one disposable database</h3>
  <p>Use this as an agent instruction, not as a shell command:</p>
  <pre><code>Use PGSandbox to create a disposable database for this task.
Run SELECT 1 AS ready in that sandbox using run_sql and show the result.
Do not use an existing application or production database.
When the check is complete, delete only the sandbox you just created.
Do not print database credentials in your explanation.</code></pre>
  <p>
    Check that the query returns <code>ready = 1</code> and that the deletion
    succeeds. TTL records expiry; it does not delete a database by itself.
    If the task is interrupted, inspect <code>list_databases</code> before an
    explicit <code>delete_database</code> or <code>cleanup_expired</code> call.
    Continue with the <a href="/docs/agent-workflows/">migration and repository workflow recipes</a>
    and the <a href="/docs/mcp-tools/">MCP tool contracts</a> once this check works.
  </p>
  <h3>If Cursor cannot find or start PGSandbox</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Confirm you ran project-scoped setup in the repository Cursor actually
      opened, not its parent directory.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the terminal finds the binary but Cursor does not, rerun setup with
      <code>--command /absolute/path/to/pgsandbox</code>, substituting the real
      installed executable path, then restart Cursor. GUI applications can
      inherit a different <code>PATH</code> from your shell.
    </li>
    <li>
      If tools appear but database creation fails, run <code>pgsandbox doctor</code>
      and fix the reported runtime issue. Changing MCP JSON will not install
      missing PostgreSQL binaries.
    </li>
    <li>
      For the managed local workflow, do not add an external admin URL to the
      MCP config. Review project config before committing it; never commit
      credentials for an external profile.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p><small>Cursor walkthrough updated October 8, 2026.</small></p>

  <h2 id="claude-code-postgres-mcp">Set up a Postgres MCP server in Claude Code</h2>
  <p>
    Register <code>pgsandbox mcp</code> as a local stdio server when Claude Code
    needs a disposable PostgreSQL database for SQL or migration checks.
    <code>pgsandbox setup --client claude-desktop</code> configures Claude Desktop,
    not Claude Code. PGSandbox v0.5.0 has no <code>--client claude-code</code> option.
  </p>
  <h3>1. Prepare the local database runtime</h3>
  <p>
    Install PGSandbox using a method above, then run these commands in your
    terminal. This prepares Postgres without writing another client's MCP config.
  </p>
  <pre class="shell"><code>pgsandbox --version
pgsandbox ensure-postgres
pgsandbox doctor
pgsandbox smoke-test</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>ensure-postgres</code> can install missing server binaries through a
    supported package manager; it is not a guarantee that every machine can
    install them automatically. Resolve any reported runtime error before
    continuing. A successful smoke test creates, queries, and deletes a sandbox;
    it does not check Claude Code's connection.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Register the stdio server in Claude Code</h3>
  <p>
    With Claude Code installed, run this from the repository where you want to
    use PGSandbox. Local scope keeps the registration private to you and limited
    to that project; it does not create a separate Postgres cluster.
  </p>
  <pre class="shell"><code>claude mcp add --transport stdio --scope local pgsandbox -- pgsandbox mcp
claude mcp get pgsandbox</code></pre>
  <p>
    Start or restart Claude Code in that repository and use <code>/mcp</code>
    to check the connection. If the executable cannot be found, use its actual
    absolute path in place of the second <code>pgsandbox</code> in the add command.
    Check an existing registration before replacing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The PGSandbox commands match the
    <a href="https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/pgsandbox/blob/v0.5.0/README.md">v0.5.0 CLI reference</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/pgsandbox/blob/v0.5.0/rust-src/setup.rs">client setup implementation</a>.
    Registration syntax and scope follow
    <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp">Claude Code's MCP documentation</a>,
    checked October 9, 2026. This is a stdio configuration recipe, not a claim
    that every Claude Code version has been tested with PGSandbox.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Verify one task through the agent</h3>
  <p>Ask Claude Code to perform this check, keeping tool approvals enabled:</p>
  <pre><code>Use PGSandbox to create a new disposable database for this check.
Run SELECT 1 AS ready with run_sql in that new sandbox.
Confirm ready = 1, then delete only the database created for this check.
Report the query and cleanup outcomes without printing credentials.
Do not connect to an existing application or production database.</code></pre>
  <p>
    If the tools load but database creation fails, troubleshoot with
    <code>pgsandbox doctor</code> rather than repeatedly changing MCP registration.
    TTL records expiry, not automatic deletion: after an interrupted task,
    inspect tracked databases and use an explicit cleanup call. See the
    <a href="/docs/mcp-tools/">tool contracts</a> and
    <a href="/docs/agent-workflows/">repository and migration workflows</a>
    for the next task. Do not supply an external admin URL for this managed-local recipe.
  </p>
  <h3>Using Claude Desktop instead?</h3>
  <p>
    Use <code>pgsandbox setup --client claude-desktop</code> and restart Desktop.
    Its configuration is user-scoped; PGSandbox rejects project scope for that
    client. This is a separate configuration path from <code>claude mcp add</code>.
  </p>
  <p><small>Claude setup walkthrough updated October 9, 2026.</small></p>

  <h2>Verify</h2>
  <pre class="shell"><code>pgsandbox doctor
pgsandbox smoke-test</code></pre>
  <p>
    Restart your MCP client after setup. In Codex, run <code>/mcp</code> to
    verify the <code>pgsandbox</code> server is available.
  </p>

  <h2>No-Docker quickstart</h2>
  <pre class="shell"><code>pgsandbox local start
pgsandbox doctor
pgsandbox smoke-test</code></pre>
  <p>
    This can run while Docker or another developer Postgres owns
    <code>5432</code>. The managed local runtime chooses a high local port such
    as <code>65432</code> or the next available port.
  </p>

  <h2>Repo workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Agents can create a sandbox, optionally call
    <code>prepare_for_repo</code> with explicit command metadata, then call
    <code>validate_schema_change</code> to run the repo's schema-change command against
    the sandbox and receive a compact schema diff.
  </p>
  <p>
    Migration and seed commands are generic argv arrays. Agents choose the
    command that fits the repository and PGSandbox injects sandbox database
    credentials through environment variables.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Install <code>initdb</code>, <code>pg_ctl</code>, and <code>postgres</code> for the managed local runtime.</li>
    <li>Install <code>pg_dump</code> and <code>pg_restore</code> for cloning and template tools.</li>
    <li>Use <code>PGSANDBOX_HOME</code> when <code>~/.pgsandbox</code> is not writable.</li>
    <li>Use <code>list_databases</code>, <code>delete_database</code>, and <code>cleanup_expired</code> for stale sandboxes.</li>
    <li>Only pass <code>--admin-url</code>, <code>PGSANDBOX_ADMIN_DATABASE_URL</code>, or <code>PGSANDBOX_CONFIG</code> for intentional external profiles.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>npm/npx status</h2>
  <p>
    Public npm/npx publishing is deferred. Use Homebrew, the GitHub install
    script, or source install until a package name, release process, and binary
    packaging are explicitly chosen.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
